  "text": "In nineteen seventy-two, Karen Spark Jones, at Cambridge, saw a flaw in how search matched words. A match on a common word counted just as much as a match on a rare one. Her fix: the fewer documents a word appears in, the more a match on it is worth. In her example of two hundred documents, a word in ninety weighs two, and a word in just three weighs seven. That's inverse document frequency, the IDF in TF-IDF, and search engines still lean on it today. The last story jumps ahead four decades.\n\nIn twenty thirteen, Tomash Mikolov and colleagues at Google released word to vec. It gives every word a point in space, and words used in similar places land close together. And directions mean something. Start at king, take away man, add woman, and the nearest word is queen. Remember great and excellent, two unrelated columns in a bag of words? Here, they're neighbors.\n\nPut it all on one line, and a thread runs through it: each new idea kept the one beneath it. Word vectors still start from tokens. Search still leans on IDF. Language models still guess the next word. And two warnings came along: a demo isn't a system, and fluent text isn't understanding. So what you learned here isn't a stage the field left behind. It's the ground everything since is built on.",
